{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>YHWH your Elohim, etc.</b> It is difficult: we do not find that they multiplied beyond what they were in Egypt by an increase that is perceptible, as is evident from the census of the second year, regarding which time he says that he said to them ״at that time״ etc. And our Sages of blessed memory in the Sifrei said, and this is their language: ״הִרְבָּה אֶתְכֶם״ — He made you great above your judges, etc., end quote. And perhaps it further intends, according to what they said in the Zohar (part 3, 111a), that by mentioning an increase of good, the power of the forces of impurity is activated to do harm, and he said there (ibid. 112a) that in every place where the name of the Most High is mentioned upon it, the evil forces have no power to dominate it. And since Moses said ״I cannot bear you alone״ etc., he is emphasizing their vast numbers, and there is in this the concern just mentioned. Therefore he mentioned His name, blessed be He, upon them and said <b>YHWH etc. has multiplied you</b>, for in this there is no opening for the harmful force, as stated there. And according to this, the word ״הִרְבָּה״ is explained according to its plain sense." "<b>And here you are today.</b> He was precise in saying <b>today</b>, to signal that the statement is divided into two time periods: one is in the second year, when he said to them ״I cannot bear alone,״ and regarding that time he says ״has multiplied you,״ and the second is at the time he is speaking with them in the fortieth year, as he was precise in saying ״and here you are today״ etc." "And the meaning of his saying <b>as the stars of the heavens</b>, he intended according to their words of blessed memory, who said (Sanhedrin 26a) that the wicked have no counting — that even though their number is great, their multitude is not accounted as a multitude. From this you learn that the counting of the righteous is attributed to increase even though they be few in number. And this is what he intended to say <b>and here you are</b> etc. <b>as the stars</b> — meaning, that after He multiplied you, you still have that here you are today as stars etc., meaning, and this matter is also a measure of increase, for in that they are like stars they are counted as an immense multitude."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The Sifrei citation (״He made you great above your judges״) reflects the standard midrashic reading that הִרְבָּה here means הִגְדִּיל, 'made great,' rather than 'multiplied.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:14:21.119631519Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}